PawnIO — signed kernel driver and bytecode modules
==================================================

Replaces the legacy WinRing0 backend. PawnIO runs sandboxed Pawn bytecode
modules inside a Microsoft-attestation-signed driver, so it loads on stock
Windows with Secure Boot and Memory Integrity (HVCI) enabled.


Provenance
----------

x64/               The x64 driver package, carved out of upstream's
                   PawnIO_setup.exe 2.1.0.0 and renamed to the names its own INF
                   and catalog reference. 149 KB against that installer's 3.2 MB.
                   sha256 PawnIO.sys f92de04e5a02256e86ffa1b4252fe669282198b7329227f40e51bca9267d133d
                   sha256 PawnIO.inf 13615792a75f85191bc3f2d19055a0292e47a1cc65f0d12b8631bcc0292b0e7f
                   sha256 PawnIO.cat b053f9301280b840a400a65a6f91eaa71f50cd74062ec17aa95f8968076ea9ee

                   The installer it came from is no longer vendored here. To
                   re-carve from a newer release, take the x64 official (not
                   -unrestricted, not ARM64) set:
                   sha256 PawnIO_setup.exe 2.1.0.0
                          a3a46226c5e2824f4cdd42be0eecbabfc672c86f7889710f5ab1e6ad385b47a0
                   Upstream downloads: https://pawnio.eu
                   Driver source:      https://github.com/namazso/PawnIO

*.bin              Signed modules from the official PawnIO.Modules 0.2.11
                   release, via LibreHardwareMonitorLib/Resources/PawnIo/.
                   Source: https://github.com/namazso/PawnIO.Modules

Upstream's installer carries x64 and ARM64 builds of both the official (signed)
and unrestricted (unsigned) editions; only the x64 official set is vendored
here. Verified on it:

    signtool verify /kp /c pawnio.cat PawnIO.sys   ->  Successfully verified
    catalog signer: CN=Microsoft Windows Hardware Compatibility Publisher
                    -> Microsoft Windows Third Party Component CA 2014
                    -> Microsoft Root Certificate Authority 2010

The driver's own embedded signature is namazso's GLOBALTRUST code-signing cert
and does NOT satisfy kernel signing policy on its own; the catalog carries the
attestation. Any driver-package install must therefore register the .cat.


Licensing
---------

LICENSE_PAWNIO  GPL-2.0 covering the driver, with the copyright holder's special
                exception permitting combination with "independent modules that
                communicate with PawnIO solely through the device IO control
                interface". Mastertech talks to PawnIO only over DeviceIoControl,
                so that exception applies and no GPL terms reach our code.

                Shipping the driver binary does carry GPLv2 section 3: source for
                the driver must be offered alongside it. The upstream repository
                above is the corresponding source for this exact version.

COPYING         LGPL-2.1 covering the .bin modules.

The exception does NOT extend to code talking over the Pawn interface: any
module loaded into PawnIO must itself be GPL/LGPL compatible. We load only
upstream's signed modules and write none of our own.


Do not modify these files
-------------------------

The modules are signature-checked by the driver (PawnIO/src/vm.cpp,
check_signature against a table of trusted keys), and the driver package is
catalog-verified. Editing any byte of a .bin or of the .inf/.cat/.sys triplet
invalidates it. Update by replacing whole files from an upstream release and
refreshing the hashes above.


Install route
-------------

pawnio-triplet     Installs x64/ through SetupAPI: SetupCopyOEMInfW to register
                   the catalog, a Root\PawnIO devnode, then
                   UpdateDriverForPlugAndPlayDevicesW. Costs 149 KB, and is
                   enabled by the hw-sensors feature the apps use. Without it
                   the backend uses an already-installed PawnIO and does nothing
                   to a machine without one.

MTECH_PAWNIO_INSTALL=none leaves an absent driver absent; =triplet is the
default and names the route explicitly.

Upstream's PawnIO_setup.exe was a second route (pawnio-installer) until
2026-09-17. It was dropped once the triplet had installed cleanly on two
machines: it cost 3.2 MB of the portable binary, and the one time it ran it was
as an unintended fallback that registered a second Root\PawnIO node beside the
first, which leaves the device unreachable until both are removed. A single
route is also what keeps that class of bug from returning.
